\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Using NSTask, your program can run another program as a subprocess and can monitor that program\'27s execution. NSTask creates a separate executable entity; it differs from NSThread in that it does not share memory space with the process that creates it. \
\fs14\
\fs28 A task operates within an environment defined by the current values for several items: the current directory, standard input, standard output, standard error, and the values of any environment variables. By default, an NSTask inherits its environment from the process that launches it. If there are any values that should be different for the task, for example, if the current directory should change, you must change the value before you launch the task. A task\'27s environment cannot be changed while it is running.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Creating an Launching an NSTask\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 There are two ways to create an NSTask. If it\'27s sufficient for the task to run in the environment that it inherits from the process that creates it, use the class method \b launchedTaskWithLaunchPath:arguments:\b0 . This method both creates and executes (launches) the task. If you need to change the task\'27s environment, create the task using \b alloc\b0  and \b init\b0 , use \b set...\b0  methods to change parts of the environment, then use the \b launch\b0  method to launch the task. For example, the following method runs tasks that take an input file and an output file as arguments. It reads these arguments, the task\'27s executable, and the current directory from text fields before it launches the task:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 - (void)runTask:(id)sender\
\{\
    NSTask *aTask = [[NSTask alloc] init];\
    NSMutableArray *args = [NSMutableArray array];\
\
    /* set arguments */\
    [args addObject:[[inputFile stringValue] lastPathComponent]];\
    [args addObject:[outputFile stringValue]];\
    [aTask setCurrentDirectoryPath:[[inputFile stringValue] \
\fi0 \tab \tab \tab stringByDeletingLastPathComponent]];\
\fi0     [aTask setLaunchPath:[taskField stringValue]];\
    [aTask setArguments:args];\
    [aTask launch];\
\}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 If you create an NSTask object in this manner, you must be sure to set the executable name using \b setLaunchPath:\b0 . If you don\'27t, an NSInvalidArgumentException is raised.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s61 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Ending an NSTask\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Normally, you want the task that you\'27ve launched to run to completion. When an NSTask exits, it posts an NSTaskDidTerminateNotification to the default notification center. You can add one of the custom objects in your program as an observer of the notification and check the task\'27s exit status (using \b terminationStatus\b0 ) in the observer method. For example:\
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 -(id)init\
\{\
    self = [super init];\
    [[NSNotificationCenter defaultCenter] addObserver:self \
\tab \tab selector:@selector(checkATaskStatus:) \
\tab \tab name:NSTaskDidTerminateNotification \
\tab \tab object:nil];\
    return self;\
\}\
\
- (void)checkATaskStatus:(NSNotification *)aNotification\
\{\
    int status = [[aNotification object] terminationStatus];\
    if (status == ATASK_SUCCESS_VALUE) \
\tab \tab NSLog(@"Task succeeded.");\
\tab else\
\tab \tab NSLog(@"Task failed.");\
\}\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 If you need to force a task to end execution, send \b terminate\b0  to the NSTask object. \
\fs14\
